# Erdos Problem #99 - certified minimax phase-star atomic gap 0.002123

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** audited `target_c_minimax_phase_star_atomic_gap_0021_2026-09-14.md`; hostile audit `target_c_minimax_phase_star_atomic_gap_0021_hostile_audit_2026-09-14.md`; audited-working Sections 163, 164, 168, 171  
**Claim class:** proof-only quantitative refinement awaiting independent audit  
**External literature:** none

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The hostile audit of the minimax phase-star theorem validates all load-bearing steps of
the `g=0.0021` proof: the weighted two-leaf deficit inequality, the best-leaf lower
bound, the one-dimensional monotonicity relaxation, the common-neighbor pruning, and
the rhombic-only transfer. The same proof is parameterized by the atomic excess `g`.
A tighter self-contained outward certificate pushes the safe round value from
`0.0021` to

\[
\boxed{g=0.002123.}
\tag{M23.1}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T-q/2\over D}
\ge 2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.002123,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
}
\tag{M23.2}
\]

and the rhombic-only transfer gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T_R\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.002123,
\qquad
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.004246.
}
\tag{M23.3}
\]

Feeding the same `g` into audited Section 171 gives the safe all-Type-II floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge {4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017327
=1.82124623797944\ldots .
}
\tag{M23.4}
\]

This is a small numerical gain over `g=0.0021`, but it is certified by the same
already-audited proof architecture and is useful because every low-`t` endpoint ledger
depends on these constants.

## 1. What changes from the audited 0.0021 proof

Nothing structural changes. In the notation of the hostile-audited minimax theorem,
put

\[
s:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\qquad
\bar A:=2s+g,
\]

\[
W_0:={4s^2\over\bar A},
\qquad
d_0:=sg+{g^2\over4}.
\]

For dominant-orbit center imbalance `z=C-W/2`, the audited weighted best-leaf theorem
reduces the contradiction to

\[
\Phi_g(z):=
{3W_0\over4}+{z\over2}
-6\sqrt{(d_0-z^2){W_0+2z\over W_0+6z}}
>{2\over\sqrt3}
\tag{M23.5}
\]

for `z<0`, with the ratio safely replaced by `1` for `z>=0`. The exact same common-
neighbor conditions must also hold.

The new verifier differs from the hardened `0.0021` certificate in two conservative
ways:

1. it derives a 45-decimal outward bracket for `s` directly from certified bounds on
   `pi` and `sqrt(3)` rather than using the coarse ten-decimal bracket; and
2. it covers `[-0.045,0.045]` by exact rational intervals of width `0.000005`.

The actual `z` domain is strictly inside this interval. On every interval the verifier
uses the same safe monotonicity directions passed by the hostile audit: left endpoint
for the raw lower term, point nearest zero for the `d_0-z^2` upper bound, and for
negative `z` the lower `W` bound plus the left endpoint for the loss ratio.

The worst certified interval is

\[
[-0.005840,-0.005835].
\]

Even there the exact outward computation gives

\[
\boxed{
\Phi_{0.002123}(z)
>{2\over\sqrt3}+1.13\times10^{-5}.
}
\tag{M23.6}
\]

The structural post-selection checks also remain strict: every possible selected
center lies below `2/sqrt(3)`, and the uniform center lower bound exceeds five times
the largest missing-pair threshold compatible with the selected degree-sum bound.
Therefore the already-audited Section-163 pruning and opposite-corner contradiction
apply unchanged.

## 2. Section-171 propagation

Set

\[
C_{146}:={4\over9}(\pi+s),
\qquad
epsilon_0:=0.0017327,
\qquad t_0:=C_{146}+\epsilon_0.
\]

For atomic gap `g=0.002123`, the Section-171 feasible detour endpoint is

\[
\delta_0
={{9\over2}\epsilon_0-g\over2}
=0.002837075.
\tag{M23.7}
\]

The exact rational companion certificate bounds the cube-root term outward and proves
that the Section-171 endpoint feasibility function is negative by more than
`3.6e-5`. Its derivative with respect to `delta` remains above `142` throughout the
feasible interval. Together with the audited monotonicity in `t`, no Type-II limit can
have `t<=t_0`, proving (M23.4).

A high-precision non-load-bearing regression puts the scalar endpoint slightly above
this safe round value; no endpoint-tight claim is made.

